WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 127: John Tyler and the Accidental Presidency: Defining Executive Succession

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.680
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:06.880
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:11.760
[Rebecca] Today we step into the presidency of our 10th chief executive, John Tyler.

4
00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:23.120
[Rebecca] In 1841, Tyler was thrust into the presidency by tragedy, facing a constitutional crisis that could have reduced the executive branch to a powerless puppet of Congress.

5
00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:39.360
[Rebecca] Nicknamed his accident by furious opponents, Tyler stood alone against party bosses, weathered the mass resignation of his entire cabinet, and established the fundamental principle of presidential succession that governs America to this day.

6
00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:45.600
[Rebecca] Here is George with the story of the accidental president.

7
00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:10.080
[George] He brought a formal letter signed by the entire cabinet informing the vice president that William Henry Harrison had died early the previous morning.

8
00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:15.440
[George] Tyler was on his knees playing marbles with his sons on the front lawn when the messenger rode up.

9
00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:17.360
[George] Tyler did not hesitate.

10
00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:29.680
[George] Without waiting for a military escort or formal instructions from Washington, he packed a small trunk, hired a fast carriage, and set out on the grueling 120 mile journey to the Capitol.

11
00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:36.320
[George] He arrived in Washington at dawn on April 6th, taking up residence at Brown's Indian Queen Hotel.

12
00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:45.600
[George] Tyler was a 51 year old patrician Virginia gentleman, tall, aristocratic, soft spoken, but possessed of an iron, stubborn will.

13
00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:58.480
[George] A lifelong strict constructionist Jeffersonian who believed deeply in states' rights, Tyler had broken with Andrew Jackson over executive tyranny during the nullification crisis and joined the loose Whig coalition.

14
00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:11.400
[George] The Whigs had placed him on the ticket in 1840 purely as a regional balance to appeal to Southern voters, never imagining for a single second that Harrison would die and this Virginia Maverick would inherit the presidency.

15
00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:19.320
[George] When Tyler met with Harrison's cabinet on the morning of April 6, he immediately confronted a profound constitutional crisis.

16
00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:35.320
[George] Secretary of State Daniel Webster politely informed Tyler that President Harrison had agreed to govern by cabinet consensus, that all major executive decisions and appointments would be decided by a majority vote of the cabinet, with the president possessing only one vote.

17
00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:40.920
[George] Tyler looked around the table at the veteran politicians and cut them off with icy finality.

18
00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:44.200
[George] I beg your pardon, gentlemen, Tyler declared.

19
00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:48.120
[George] I am very glad to have in my cabinet gentlemen of your abilities.

20
00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:53.080
[George] But I can never consent to being dictated to as to what I shall or shall not do.

21
00:02:53.080 --> 00:02:57.480
[George] I am the president, and I shall be responsible for my administration.

22
00:02:57.480 --> 00:03:01.000
[George] When you think proper to do as I wish, you may stay.

23
00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:04.840
[George] When you wish otherwise, your resignations will be accepted.

24
00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:08.600
[George] Then came the explosive question of Tyler's official title.

25
00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:18.360
[George] Article II of the Constitution was ambiguously worded, stating that upon the death of a president, the powers and duties shall devolve on the vice president.

26
00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:29.080
[George] Congressional Whig leaders like Henry Clay argued that Tyler was merely a regent, an acting president, holding the powers of the office temporarily while remaining the vice president.

27
00:03:29.080 --> 00:03:38.760
[George] Tyler saw the mortal danger in this interpretation: an acting president would have no independent mandate and would be a helpless puppet at the mercy of Congress.

28
00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:44.920
[George] Tyler insisted that by constitutional operation, he was the president of the United States in full.

29
00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:55.040
[George] He immediately summoned Chief Judge William Cranch of the Federal District Court and took the presidential oath of office, becoming the tenth president of the United States.

30
00:03:55.040 --> 00:04:00.320
[George] Tyler's bold self-elevation ignited furious indignation on Capitol Hill.

31
00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:12.960
[George] Former President John Quincy Adams, now a representative from Massachusetts, wrote bitterly in his diary that Tyler's assumption of the title of President was a radical violation of the Constitution.

32
00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:18.800
[George] Whig newspapers across the country mocked him as his accidency and the executive by chance.

33
00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:33.280
[George] When letters arrived at the White House addressed to His Excellency John Tyler, Vice President, Acting President, Tyler ordered the male clerks to return them unopened, marked with a handwritten note, to whom it may concern.

34
00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:39.280
[George] There is no such officer as vice president-acting president known to the laws of the United States.

35
00:04:39.280 --> 00:04:45.920
[George] When Congress convened in special session in May, both houses debated whether to address him as president.

36
00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:53.040
[George] After bitter argument, both the Senate and the House conceded and addressed Tyler as the President of the United States.

37
00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:58.080
[George] This decisive action became known to constitutional history as the Tyler Precedent.

38
00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:03.840
[George] When Zachary Taylor died in 1850, Millard Fillmore took the oath as president.

39
00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:15.600
[George] When Lincoln was assassinated in 1865, Andrew Johnson became president, and when Kennedy was murdered in 1963, Lyndon Johnson took the oath aboard Air Force One.

40
00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:20.800
[George] In each case, succession was instantaneous, peaceful, and unquestioned.

41
00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:29.920
[George] In 1967, the nation finally formalized Tyler's courageous precedent into constitutional law with the ratification of the 25th Amendment.

42
00:05:30.040 --> 00:05:39.960
[George] Having established his constitutional legitimacy, Tyler immediately collided with the undisputed titan of the Whig Party, Senator Henry Clay of Kentucky.

43
00:05:39.960 --> 00:05:53.480
[George] Clay had spent a decade building the Whig Party's signature legislative agenda, known as the American System, a powerful national bank, high protective tariffs, and massive federal spending on roads and canals.

44
00:05:53.480 --> 00:06:03.560
[George] Clay viewed Harrison's election as the long-awaited triumph of his program, and he had no intention of allowing an accidental president from Virginia to stand in his way.

45
00:06:03.560 --> 00:06:16.440
[George] Clay boldly announced his legislative program and pushed a bill through Congress to charter the fiscal bank of the United States, a resurrected third national bank modeled on the institution Andrew Jackson had destroyed.

46
00:06:16.440 --> 00:06:20.680
[George] Clay assumed Tyler would rubber stamp the bill under party pressure.

47
00:06:20.680 --> 00:06:24.360
[George] He told friends, Tyler dares not resist us.

48
00:06:24.360 --> 00:06:28.680
[George] I will drive him before me, Clay completely misjudged his man.

49
00:06:28.680 --> 00:06:43.400
[George] John Tyler was a lifelong constitutional purist who had voted against national banks for thirty years, believing they created unconstitutional financial monopolies and placed the sovereignty of the states under the heel of wealthy private financiers.

50
00:06:43.400 --> 00:06:54.680
[George] When the bank bill reached his desk, Whig delegations pleaded with Tyler to sign, warning that a veto would destroy the Whig Party and ignite an unstoppable civil war within the government.

51
00:06:54.680 --> 00:06:58.360
[George] Tyler responded with quiet, aristocratic granite.

52
00:06:58.360 --> 00:07:04.760
[George] I will die before I will surrender the principles of my life and the constitutional oath I have sworn to God.

53
00:07:04.760 --> 00:07:11.960
[George] On August 16, 1841, Tyler returned the bank bill to the Senate with a stinging presidential veto.

54
00:07:11.960 --> 00:07:19.440
[George] Clay was beside himself with volcanic rage, pacing the Senate floor and shouting that Tyler was an apostate traitor.

55
00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:29.520
[George] Whig leaders quickly passed a second, slightly modified bank bill, attempting to trick Tyler by calling it a fiscal corporation and masking its banking powers.

56
00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:32.320
[George] But Tyler saw right through the deception.

57
00:07:32.320 --> 00:07:37.360
[George] On September 9, 1841, Tyler vetoed the second bank bill.

58
00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:45.200
[George] Henry Clay's dream of a resurrected national bank was permanently crushed, and the battle lines were drawn for total political war.

59
00:07:45.200 --> 00:07:50.640
[George] Tyler's second veto detonated an unprecedented political explosion in Washington.

60
00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:56.880
[George] On the night of September 9, an armed mob of hundreds of drunken Whig partisans marched on the White House.

61
00:07:56.880 --> 00:08:09.360
[George] They shouted obscenities, fired pistols into the air, and burned an effigy of President Tyler on the White House lawn, throwing stones through the windows while Tyler and his family sat in darkness inside.

62
00:08:09.360 --> 00:08:15.760
[George] It was the most violent demonstration against a sitting president on the executive grounds in American history.

63
00:08:15.760 --> 00:08:19.840
[George] Tyler formed the first White House police detail in response.

64
00:08:19.840 --> 00:08:25.520
[George] Two days later, Henry Clay executed an unprecedented political decapitation strike.

65
00:08:25.520 --> 00:08:30.640
[George] He orchestrated the coordinated mass resignation of Harrison's entire cabinet.

66
00:08:30.640 --> 00:08:51.200
[George] On Saturday, September 11, 1841, five cabinet secretaries, including the Secretary of the Treasury, the Secretary of War, and the Attorney General, marched into the White House and handed Tyler their formal resignations, intending to paralyze the executive branch and force Tyler to resign the presidency.

67
00:08:51.200 --> 00:09:02.920
[George] Only Secretary of State Daniel Webster refused to quit, partly out of personal rivalry with Clay, and partly because he was in the middle of delicate border negotiations with Great Britain.

68
00:08:59.840 --> 00:09:05.400
[George] Tyler remained totally unflappable.

69
00:09:05.640 --> 00:09:14.040
[George] Within 48 hours, he sent a complete slate of new cabinet nominees to the Senate, all respected Southern and Western jurists.

70
00:09:14.040 --> 00:09:25.160
[George] Two days after that, the Whig Congressional Caucus met and issued an extraordinary public manifesto, formally expelling the sitting President of the United States from the Whig Party.

71
00:09:25.160 --> 00:09:28.440
[George] John Tyler had become a president without a party.

72
00:09:28.440 --> 00:09:34.520
[George] For the remainder of his four years in office, John Tyler occupied the loneliest post in the world.

73
00:09:34.520 --> 00:09:43.640
[George] He was a president reviled by the Whigs as a traitor to the party that elected him, and distrusted by the Democrats who remembered his past opposition to Jackson.

74
00:09:43.640 --> 00:10:01.480
[George] Whig newspapers printed death notices for the administration, and the Whig dominated House of Representatives, led by former President John Quincy Adams, launched the first formal impeachment inquiry against a president in American history, charging him with gross abuse of the veto power.

75
00:10:01.480 --> 00:10:05.960
[George] The impeachment resolution failed, but the hostility never abated.

76
00:10:05.960 --> 00:10:13.880
[George] Yet, despite this total isolation, Tyler's administration achieved remarkable triumphs, working with Daniel Webster.

77
00:10:13.880 --> 00:10:29.960
[George] Tyler negotiated the landmark Webster Rashburton Treaty of 1842 with Great Britain, peacefully settling the disputed northeastern border between Maine and Canada and establishing joint Anglo American patrols to suppress the illegal African slave trade.

78
00:10:29.960 --> 00:10:47.520
[George] Tyler ended the costly Second Seminole War in Florida, signed the landmark treaty of Wangia opening American trade with Qing Dynasty China, and extended the Monroe Doctrine to the Hawaiian Islands, warning European empires that Hawaii lay within the sphere of American protection.

79
00:10:47.840 --> 00:10:54.320
[George] Tyler demonstrated that the executive branch was not dependent on party patronage or congressional favor.

80
00:10:54.320 --> 00:11:03.840
[George] Armed with the presidential veto and constitutional authority, a resolute president could stand alone and govern a great republic through sheer willpower.

81
00:11:03.840 --> 00:11:11.200
[George] John Tyler's presidency was one of the most turbulent in American history, but its constitutional legacy was profound.

82
00:11:11.200 --> 00:11:19.520
[George] By staring down Henry Clay and refusing to be reduced to a congressional clerk, Tyler saved the independence of the American presidency.

83
00:11:19.520 --> 00:11:35.200
[George] Had Tyler yielded, had he accepted the title of acting president or surrendered executive authority to cabinet consensus, the United States would have drifted toward a parliamentary system, where the executive is merely a temporary creature of legislative whim.

84
00:11:35.200 --> 00:11:45.440
[George] Instead, Tyler established that the president is the independent, co-equal representative of the entire American people, regardless of how he arrives at the office.

85
00:11:45.440 --> 00:11:51.280
[George] He demonstrated that the constitutional oath is taken to the nation, not to a political party.

86
00:11:51.280 --> 00:11:59.760
[George] Tyler would cap his embattled presidency with an audacious geopolitical triumph, bringing the Republic of Texas into the American Union.

87
00:11:59.760 --> 00:12:15.760
[George] But his finest hour was the quiet courage he showed in April 1841, when he took the oath as president and declared that under the Constitution of the United States, there are no accidental leaders, only presidents sworn to protect the liberty of a free people.

88
00:12:16.240 --> 00:12:25.920
[Rebecca] John Tyler established the vital precedent of vice presidential succession, ensuring that the American presidency remains stable and continuous in times of tragedy.

89
00:12:25.920 --> 00:12:44.920
[Rebecca] Next week, we explore John Tyler's crowning geopolitical crusade, the annexation of Texas in 1844, the secret treaties, the threat of British imperial expansion, and the masterly constitutional maneuver that added nearly 400,000 square miles to the United States.

90
00:12:44.920 --> 00:12:49.560
[Rebecca] Until then, thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

